\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  -3: Won Hits 5.5-Mo High; NT$ Higher \b0\par\par\uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1999\par 12:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere the South Korean won (KRW) leapt to its highest level since early January after local wire services reported remarks by Finance Minister Kang Bong-kyun that "the government doesn't favor direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market." \par \par \uc2  After two months of consistent dollar buying by state-run banks and, more recently, the Bank of Korea, traders in Seoul interpreted the remarks as implying that the government was backing away from its policy of suppressing the won to safeguard export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  In response Korean exporters holding U.S. dollar revenues rushed to convert them into the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing the dollar to an intraday low of KRW1,156.50, the lowest it has been offered since January 8. \par \par \uc2  By the end of local trading, the U.S. dollar had recovered slightly, closing at KRW1,159.40, down from KRW1,165.50 at Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  The new Taiwan dollar continued to grind higher in the face of dollar purchases by the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , setting a fresh four-month high in intraday trading. \par \par \uc2  At the close the U.S. dollar was quoted at NT$32.32.348, down fractionally from NT$32.349 the day before. \par \par \uc2  Had it not been for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could easily have fallen below NT$32.000, said dealers. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action helped support the U.S. dollar against the Singapore dollar and the Thai baht (THB). \par \par \uc2  Toward the end of Asian dealing, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted against the Singapore dollar at S$1.7065, down a touch from S$1.7090 late Monday but still above the level of S$1.7000 at which the market fears \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Against the baht the U.S. dollar held firm through Asian trading, only to drop sharply in European hours, falling below the THB36.6000 level at which some dealers had expected the Bank of Thailand to enter the market. \par \par \uc2  Around 0930 GMT the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at THB36.5850, down from THB36.8900 late in Asian trading Monday. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the U.S. dollar ended trading against the Philippine peso at PHP37.855, down from PHP37.910 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 22-06-99 \par \par \uc2  1031GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6m04ta5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
