\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  -2: Correlation With Yen Breaks Down \b0\par\par\uc2 250 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 1999\par 11:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Toward the end of local hours the U.S. dollar was quoted against the Singapore dollar at S$1.7251, down from S$1.7317 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Against the Thai baht (THB), the U.S. dollar was at THB37.0650, down from THB37.1100 late in Asia on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The recent breakdown of the long-standing correlation between regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the yen has left traders with little idea which direction markets are likely to take over the coming week. \par \par \uc2  "You can't take the yen as a guideline any more," complained one dealer, adding that market players would be closely watching local central banks for their trading cues. \par \par \uc2  "The important thing for the authorities around the region is to stabilize their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. While central bankers may be prepared to tolerate moves of a couple of percentage points either side of current exchange rates, he argued that they would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check any extreme fluctuations. \par \par \uc2  Other dealers agreed, saying that they would be wary of indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the U.S. dollar fall to levels approaching S$1.7150. Equally, the authority would be likely to check any rapid appreciation toward S$1.7350, they added. \par \par \uc2  The baht is thought likely to be similarly range-bound in coming sessions, with the U.S. dollar continuing to trade in a THB37.05 to THB37.15 band. \par \p