\par\par \b \uc2  SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  -3: S$ Stronger; US$ Support At S$1.70 \b0\par\par\uc2 183 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 1999\par 11:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar edged slightly higher Wednesday, although the U.S. dollar remained above the S$1.7000 support level below which many traders believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  At the local close, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7005, down from S$1.7058 late Tuesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Both the Philippine peso and the New Taiwan dollar closed weaker, as market participants covered short U.S. dollar positions in response to the weak tone of the yen in Asian trading and falls in local stock markets. \par \par \uc2  At the close of trading on the Philippine Dealing System, the U.S. dollar was quoted at 37.790 pesos, up from PHP37.770 at the end of Tuesday's session. \par \par \uc2  In the Taipei market, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at NT$32.749, up from NT$32.723 the day before. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 65 421 4808; tom.holland@cor.dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 05-05-99 \par \par \uc2  0933GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv5503d51\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
