\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dollar Up Early; Support For Good Economic Data \b0\par\par\uc2 231 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Mai 1999\par 03:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar is stronger in early trading compared with levels late Friday, as the market digested a menu of good economic figures announced over the weekend by Prime Minister Goh Chok Tong. \par \par \uc2  But with Japan's and Thailand's markets closed due to holidays, trading Monday is expected to be quiet, with players watching for signs the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen any further. \par \par \uc2  About 0125 GMT (9:25 p.m. EDT Sunday), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.6956, down from S$1.7016 in late Asian trading Friday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should have some support at these levels, traders said, while it's overall range for Monday's session is seen at about S$1.6930 to S$1.7000. \par \par \uc2  "People are concerned that this level is MAS territory," a senior trader in Singapore said. "People are reluctant to push it lower." \par \par \uc2  Giving the Singapore dollar support, over the weekend Goh said the Singapore economy grew by 1.2% in the first quarter and raised the government's 1999 outlook to 0.0%-2.0% growth. He also said unemployment at the end of the first quarter was 3.9%, down from 4.4% at the end of 1998. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 03-05-99 \par \par \uc2  0136GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv53039uf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
