\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dollar Seen Range-Bound Early; Watching Yen Moves \b0\par\par\uc2 192 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 1999\par 04:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar is expected to stay range-bound in early Monday trade after strengthening slightly over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  With no surprising domestic news to move the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Singapore dollar is expected to remain hostage to yen movements on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, traders said. \par \par \uc2  About 0155 GMT (9:55 p.m. EDT Sunday), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7061, down from S$1.7100 late Friday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  With players watching for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cool the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's strength, support for the U.S. dollar is seen around S$1.7050, a trader in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar sell orders, particularly from equity funds, begin to show up when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  approaches S$1.7100. \par \par \uc2  On the local money market, the interbank overnight offer rat was at 0.750%, down from 1.375 late Friday, while the three-month rate was at 1.375%, up from 1.250% late Friday. \par \par \uc2  -By Roger Malone; 65/421 4800; rmalone@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 26-04-99 \par \par \uc2  0207GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv4q035nj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
