\par\par \b \uc2  SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Strengthen; Won Up Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 277 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 1999\par 10:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--Most East Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened on Friday, lifted higher against the U.S. dollar by buoyant investor sentiment. \par \par \uc2  But after seeing regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise by amounts varying between 1% and 2.5% over the week, traders and analysts were divided on whether the bull run can continue in coming sessions. \par \par \uc2  If funds continue to flow into regional stock markets at the same rate, there will undoubtedly be more upward pressure on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But many traders believe further appreciation is likely to prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In Korea, the authorities have already been buying U.S. dollars in the market for weeks now, in an attempt to prevent won (KRW) strength eroding export competitiveness. Although many traders and analysts believe the U.S. dollar would now be as low as KRW1,150 without government-directed buying from state-owned banks, few expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to come to a halt in the near future. \par \par \uc2  "Without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the dollar supply would far exceed dollar demand, but the government prefers to keep the won weak, at about KRW1,200," to the U.S. dollar, said Ji-won Lim, an economist at J.P. Morgan in Seoul. "This is consistent with the Bank of Korea's expansionary policy, so \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue for some time to come." \par \par \uc2  At the close of domestic trading in Seoul on Friday, the U.S dollar was quoted at KRW1,216.90, driven down from KRW1,218.00 at Thursday's close despite heavy buying from the state-run Export-Import Bank. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 16-04-99 \par \par \uc2  0829GMT \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv4g02wn5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
