\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia: Fall In Singapore Reserves May Have Been Euro Bet \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 926 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 06:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--A US$1.76 billion drop in Singapore's foreign reserves has some people wondering whether the authorities have discretely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But others speculate that the drop could suggest that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost heavily by banking on a strong euro. \par \par \uc2  Falling reserves, some analysts argue, are clear evidence that the MAS, an organization famed as much for its opacity as its plentifully stuffed coffers, has quietly sold U.S. dollars over the past month to help the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  If that's true, they contend, then the market consensus that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to retest its 1998 high of S$1.80 is far wide of the mark. \par \par \uc2  But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not the only possible explanation for the dip in Singapore's foreign reserves. If market whispers are correct, toward the end of last year the MAS bought heavily into the strong euro story then being touted by so many single-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bulls, and converted a healthy portion of its reserves into shiny new euros. \par \par \uc2  Since then of course, the European single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost some of its luster, dropping 7.7% against the U.S. dollar from its launch. That, along with February's the slide in the yen, is more likely to account for the missing US$1.76 billion, say skeptics. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell 2.4% to US$72.38 billion at the end of February, from US$74.14 at the end of January, according to figures released last Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "This shows that the MAS is determinedly selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is as clear a signal as it is possible to get that the MAS is aggressively defending current levels," says Prasenjit Basu, a regional economist at Credit Suisse First Boston in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Over the same period the Singapore dollar slipped by 2% against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the U.S. dollar rising to end February at S$1.7250 compared S$1.6900 at the end of January. \par \par \uc2  Even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar still fell, Basu believes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was necessary to ensure a controlled, gradual depreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  With Singapore dollar short term interest rates consistently below 2% over the last two months, the Singapore dollar has become the funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of choice in Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Those who are able have sold the Singapore dollar short to fund long positions in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  including the Thai baht and the Philippine peso, as well as in the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  To offset the demand to sell the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Basu, the MAS has been forced to sell U.S. dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  And this willingness of the MAS to part with some of reserves indicates that market players have been overly pessimistic in their forecasts for the Singapore dollar's exchange rate, he argues. \par \par \uc2  "I disagree with the consensus year-end forecast of S$1.85. The Singapore dollar won't go much further than S$1.76," to the U.S. dollar, Basu says. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hovered around the S$1.72 level Tuesday, down from S$1.7260 late in Asia Monday. \par \par \uc2  Valuation Effects Thought Most Likely To Explain Fall \par \par \uc2  Other analysts, however, question whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have caused the hole in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the exchange rate moves in February warranted a US$1.7 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says one. "Valuation effects are a more convincing argument." \par \par \uc2  With a current account surplus running at around $15 billion and with its enviable status as a net creditor nation, Singapore's trade and investment flows are not feasible explanations for the drop in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. But valuation effects, the drop in the U.S. dollar value of the reserves caused by the depreciation of other reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are a possible cause. \par \par \uc2  But valuation effects could only be the explanation if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  was holding a relatively large proportion of its reserves in the euro and the yen, which depreciated by 3.2% and 3.3% respectively against the U.S. dollar during February. \par \par \uc2  Assuming that the reserves are split solely between U.S. dollar, euro and yen, that would mean over two thirds of Singapore's foreign reserves would have to have been held in euro and yen-denominated instruments to account for their 2.4% decline in U.S. dollar terms. \par \par \uc2  Few observers believe the MAS would hold anything like that proportion of its reserves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  other than the U.S. dollar. Nevertheless, sources familiar with the workings of the authority say that dollar, euro and yen are not held in the roughly 45%-40%-15% split current trade relationships would imply. \par \par \uc2  "The composition of the reserves is not cast in stone," says one knowledgeable source. "The MAS is just like any other fund manager. It forecasts rates of return and makes its allocations accordingly." \par \par \uc2  If the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  called this year's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market wrongly, it would hardly be the only one. Nevertheless requests for official comment elicited no response on Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  The MAS, an organization whose press officers have been known to respond to media enquiries with a terse "No comment, and that's off the record," was remaining characteristically tight-lipped. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 65 421 4808 \par \par \uc2  tom.holland@cor.dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Corrected 0714GMT) \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 16-03-99 \par \par \uc2  0505GMT \par \par \uc2  4258 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv3g022zx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
