\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Steady Mid-Afternoon; Range-Bound Trade Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 321 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 1998\par 08:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is mostly steady against the U.S. dollar in mid-afternoon Asian dealings Monday with range-bound trade expected in the short term, market players said. \par \par \uc2  On the one hand, the stock market rally, improved sentiment in the region, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the forthcoming move by the government to cut business costs are lending some strength to the Singapore dollar, said market players. \par \par \uc2  On the other hand, fear of the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to smooth out fluctuations is seen halting potential gains by the Singapore dollar, they said. Also, comments by a senior Singapore banker earlier Monday that prime lending rates could ease about 25 to 50 basis points before the end of the year are seen weakening interest in the Singapore dollar, they said. \par \par \uc2  "The trend points to the (U.S.) dollar trading between S$1.6150 and S$1.6300," said one market player, adding that "we think the dollar-Sing (U.S. dollar versus the Singapore dollar) is very range bound." \par \par \uc2  A regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Japanese bank said a technical correction for the U.S. dollar should bring the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher to S$1.65 in the short term. Subsequently, the U.S. dollar should trend downward against the Singapore dollar, he said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0730 GMT, the dollar was trading around S$1.6192, almost steady from S$1.6190 at 0135 GMT early in Asian trading but down from S$1.6255 around the same time Friday. \par \par \uc2  In the local money market, the overnight rate on Singapore dollar deposits was quoted at 0.750%, down from 1.25% late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The three-month interbank rate was 2.0%, down from 2.625% late Friday. \par \par \uc2  -Joseph Rajendran; 65 421 4800 \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 02-11-98 \par \par \uc2  0748GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917dub200p3j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
