\par\par \b \uc2  SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 /Early -2: Rupiah Falls On Rising Tension \b0\par\par\uc2 218 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 1998\par 03:01\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Trading in the Singapore dollar is also subdued, with the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to remain range-bound, according to a trader at a local bank. \par \par \uc2  With profit-takers expected to cap the U.S. dollar's upside around S$1.6350, and fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  supporting the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at S$1.6200, few expect the market to break out of that range, he said. \par \par \uc2  At 0150 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.6246, up fractionally from S$1.6235 toward the end of Asian trading the day before. \par \par \uc2  An upsurge of political tension in Indonesia, meanwhile, has triggered a drop in the rupiah (IDR) early Thursday, which has seen the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall close to the bottom of its recent trading range. \par \par \uc2  About 8,000 students demonstrated near parliament Wednesday, saying they are against a forthcoming government assembly that will establish guidelines for elections in 1999. \par \par \uc2  Concerns that the protests may escalate have seen the U.S. dollar rise to IDR7,850 currently from IRD7,700 late Wednesday. Most traders, however, expect the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be capped around IDR8,000. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 65 421 4800 \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires  10-28-98 \par \par \uc2  2101EST \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916duat02gg8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\pi