\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore dollar rides crest of yen surge. \b0\par\par\uc2 439 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 1998\par 05:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 8 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar was among the biggest beneficiaries of the yen's spectacular overnight rise as hedge funds and investors who had been selling it as a proxy for Asia cut their short positions, dealers said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , recently a victim of its own liquidity as trade in the other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  withered, gained over two percent from late Wednesday levels in line with the dizzying drop in dollar/yen. \par \par \uc2  It stood at 1.6400/20 to the U.S. dollar at 0240 GMT after briefly firming above the 1.6400 barrier, compared with about 1.67 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was holding above 120 yen in Tokyo morning trade after tumbling to a 12-month low of around 118.95 yen overnight as hedge funds, unwinding short yen positions, took the view that Japan would implement new measures to revitalise its banking system. \par \par \uc2  "The Sing is just riding on the back of the yen's movement. I reckon there are still a lot of sellers who missed the boat last night, but we should see more consolidative trading today," said the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at an Asian bank. \par \par \uc2  He said the the Sing dollar was likely to face some resistance around 1.62 per U.S. dollar and should trade in a range of 1.62 to 1.67 in the next few days. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said hedge funds and interbank players had been shorting the Singapore dollar as liquidity dwindled in the Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah and after Malaysia imposed stringent controls on the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  "Relative to the rest of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's very easy to run a short position in the Sing dollar because interest rates are rather low," said Chiang Yao Chye, head of Asia-Pacific research at CIBC in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "That's been a very popular trade for some time and a lot of these short-Sing/long-dollar positions are just getting out. As long as dollar/yen drops further, you're going to see more of that," he added. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) to slow the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise as it was in line with the overall trend for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "With the yen strengthening by nearly 10 percent, and the Sing is only up by some two to three percent, I don't think the MAS is uncomfortable," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  - Sonali Desai (65) 870-3090; Fax (65) 870-3787 \par \par \uc2  - Email: asia.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 @reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113dua801i2j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
