\par\par \b \uc2  SINGAPORE STOCKS - Factors to watch - Sept 3. \b0\par\par\uc2 390 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 02:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 3 (Reuters) - Following are some of the main factors likely to affect Singapore's stocks on Thursday: \par \par \uc2  OUTLOOK \par \par \uc2  * The sacking of Malaysian Deputy Prime Minister and Finance Minister Anwar Ibrahim is likely to be the market's main talking point and will add to uncertainty over comapanies with Malaysian exposure. \par \par \uc2  * Malaysia was already set to be the dominant factor in a continuing swirl of questions and confusion after the country on Tuesday imposed controls on the ringgit and restrictions on share trading in a bid to insulate the economy from contagion effects of regional economic problems. \par \par \uc2  * Markets across the Americas ended mixed as U.S. stocks sputtered lower in the final minutes of the session. Uncertainty still reigned on Wall Street and even at the day's peaks, U.S. investors expressed none of the unbridled optimism of recent years. \par \par \uc2  * Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday morning said it has eased monetary policy further by cutting its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate and liquidity assets ratio requirement. \par \par \uc2  * \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market Committee had agreed on Wednesday on a rate of four Malaysian ringgit to one U.S. dollar for the settlement of outstanding ringgit positions in the offshore market, Singapore stste television reported. \par \par \uc2  * Continued volatility expected in stocks linked to the Morgan Stanley Capital International Index (MSCI) ahead of the launch of SIMEX-MSCI futures index on September 7. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS TO WATCH \par \par \uc2  * Singapore Bus Services  after reporting a net profit of S$16.18 million in the first half. \par \par \uc2  * Diversified engineering and trading company Lindeteves-Jacoberg after posting a net profit rise of 31 percent in the first half. \par \par \uc2  * Hotel and leisure group Apollo Enterprises  after sinking deeper into red in the first half. \par \par \uc2  * Banks and companies with exposures to Malaysia. \par \par \uc2  * Malaysian shares traded on the over-the-counter Clob in Singapore after Malaysia decided not to recognise those trades. \par \par \uc2  MARKET CLOSINGS \par \par \uc2  * On Wednesday, the Straits Times Industrials index (STII) ended up 0.55 percent, or 4.52 points, at 827.85 points. \par \par \uc2  * The Dow Jones Industrial average lost 45.06 points, or almost 0.6 percent, to close at 7,782.37 on Wednesday, on profit taking after Tuesday's 288-point rise. \par \par \uc2  - Singapore newsroom (65) 870-3080; Fax (65) 776-8112 \par \par \uc2  - Email: singapore.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du93022un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
