\par\par \b \uc2  SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  -2: Singapore Dlr Sold Vs Ringgit, Baht \b0\par\par\uc2 311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 1998\par 12:02\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Many believe that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and money\par markets for months to massage the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lower, in an\par effort to reverse the erosion in export competitiveness\par caused by the depreciation of other regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    Late on Monday the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7677, up\par from S$1.7575 late in Asia on Friday. Of the action there\par was, much took place in the regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cross-markets,\par according to traders.\par    "People are selling the Singapore dollar against the\par other regionals," said the head of trading at one U.S. bank\par in the island republic, although he added: "The amounts are\par not significant".\par    Offshore players, he explained, are selling the Singapore\par dollar short against the U.S. dollar in the forward market.\par At the same time they are selling U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  dollars against the\par ringgit and the baht in offsetting transactions, which leave\par them benefiting from the gaping interest rate differentials\par between the Singapore dollar and other regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    Late on Monday the Singapore dollar interest rate implied\par by the three month US$/S$ forward stood at 5.41%. In contrast,\par the equivalent ringgit and baht rates stood at 19.69% and\par 19.09% respectively.\par    Cross-market buying failed to buoy either the baht or the\par ringgit, however, with both finishing Asian trading hours\par lower against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    "Stock markets are down across the region, and the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are playing catch up, as negative sentiment spills\par over," said one regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader at another U.S.\par bank.\par    Late in the Asian day, the U.S. dollar was quoted at\par 4.2225 ringgit (MYR), up from MYR4.2050 towards the end of\par Friday's trading session.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  08-24-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0602EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du8o01zw6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
