\par\par \b \uc2  SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  -3: Ringgit,  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Edge Higher \b0\par\par\uc2 309 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 1998\par 13:10\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Despite market participants' wariness of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a\par fresh bout of dollar buying against Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could be\par set off by a trigger event like the collapse of a major\par Japanese bank, warns Gittler.\par    "The yen could collapse any day, dragging the regional\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down with it. Until Japan's banking reform bills\par are passed, there is a very serious danger overhanging\par Southeast Asia. I would not be a buyer of regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par right now," he said.\par    Against the ringgit the U.S. dollar ended Asian trading\par at 4.2050 ringgit (MYR), down from MYR4.2350 the day before.\par The U.S. dollar is likely to find support at around MYR2.1800\par to MYR2.2000, with bids emerging at those levels, say\par traders.\par    Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dropped\par to S$1.7565 late in Asian trading, down from S$1.7689 late on\par Tuesday.\par    Traders described good two-way interest, with local banks\par seen bidding to buy U.S. dollars around S$1.7550.\par    "Every time it (the U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  dollar) dips down, it pops\par straight back up," said one trader at a U.S. bank in\par Singapore, suggesting the local banks may have been acting on\par behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore to check\par excessive strength in the Singapore \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    The MAS is widely believed in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par to have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  repeatedly in recent weeks in an attempt\par to push the Singapore dollar lower.\par    Although the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has depreciated by about 20%\par against the U.S. dollar since the start of the Asian crisis\par last July, over the same period it has risen against every\par freely-floating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in Asia, raising worries that an\par overly strong Singapore dollar is rendering exports\par uncompetitive.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  08-19-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0710EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du8j020ob\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
