\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  selling own  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  - traders. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 1998\par 19:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Reuters) - It would seem rather odd for a Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be selling its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after more than a year of market turmoil and grinding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation. \par \par \uc2  But that is what \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) has been doing in the last couple of weeks. \par \par \uc2  They say local banks, which often act on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have been spotted buying the U.S. dollar each time the Singapore dollar shows signs of strengthening. \par \par \uc2  "Every time the dollar/Sing drops too much, the local banks are in to support it," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "Singapore needs to not have too strong a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because of export competitiveness," she added. \par \par \uc2  But analysts say that although there are compelling arguments for a weaker Singapore dollar, including the oft-mentioned export competitiveness concern, the MAS is highly unlikely to actively depreciate the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I don't buy the idea that the government is out there trying to depreciate the Singapore dollar to 1.80 or even 2.00 (to the U.S. dollar)," said Daniel Lian, head of Asian markets research at ANZ Investment Bank. \par \par \uc2  "If there's any activity (buying of dollar/Sing), the volume has been extremely light. I don't think they're in a mood to sharply depreciate the Sing dollar," he added. \par \par \uc2  An MAS spokesman said the bank does not comment on its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar stood at around 1.72 to the U.S. dollar in late Asian trade on Friday. It has not strengthened above the 1.70 level since July 21. \par \par \uc2  Most analysts attributed the apparent MAS purchases of U.S. dollars to routine smoothing of sharp moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market or to the bank adjusting its own portfolio. \par \par \uc2  "The argument of trying to replenish reserves at the margin makes more sense because if you're trying to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you would typically catch it on a weakening trend," said Paul Alapat, senior economist at Indosuez W.I.Carr. \par \par \uc2  "The direction would be to tolerate some weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in line with its trading partners and to that extent the Singapore dollar has some catching up to do," he said. \par \par \uc2  ANZ's Lian said the 1.66-1.75 region would be a suitable trading range for the Singapore dollar unless other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the yen, fell sharply. \par \par \uc2  But given the Singapore dollar's relative resilience compared with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its export rivals and a benign domestic inflation scenario, analysts say there is potential for more depreciation. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar is down by about 17 percent against the U.S. dollar since the start of the Asian crisis last July, while the Malaysian ringgit and Thai baht have lost between 35 and 40 percent. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8201i6n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
