\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dollar Rebounds Early From Monday's Sell-Off \b0\par\par\uc2 372 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 1998\par 04:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar has strengthened in early trading Tuesday morning, rebounding after Monday's sell-off. \par \par \uc2  "People were caught long (U.S. dollars), so now they are covering," said a trader at a U.K. bank. \par \par \uc2  Market participants sold the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday in response to newspaper reports of heavy losses suffered by a Brunei-based investment company, Amedeo, which is controlled by Prince Jefri Bolkiah, brother of the ruling Sultan. The Singapore dollar has rallied sharply early Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was targeted because Brunei operates a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board linking its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the Singapore dollar. Any adverse effects of the losses - reported to be worth as much as $16 billion - on the Brunei economy would therefore have an impact on the Singapore dollar exchange rate, traders reasoned at the time. \par \par \uc2  "People have looked again at the news out yesterday and realized that it was probably vastly exaggerated. Given the potential of the yen to strengthen from here, they are buying the Singapore dollar back again to take profits from yesterday's move," said the head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a Japanese bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  At 0200 GMT (10:00 p.m. Monday EDT) the U.S. dollar has slipped back to trade at S$1.7060, down from S$1.7128 late on Monday. Bids below S$1.7000 are seen likely to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the time being, and with the yen relatively steady, the U.S. dollar is seen ranging between S$1.6990 and S$1.7090. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the ringgit is at S$0.4088, little changed from S$0.4087 late the day before. Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to support the ringgit above S$0.4000 in the short term, say traders. \par \par \uc2  In Singapore's local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated money market, the overnight rate on Singapore dollar deposits has risen sharply to 4.50%-5.00%, from 1.50%-2.00% late on Monday. The three-month rate remains flat at 6.00%-6.25%. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 65 421 4800 \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 07-07-98 \par \par \uc2  0215GMT \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du7705p68\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
