\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Late-AM 2:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Feared Later In Week \b0\par\par\uc2 192 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 1998\par 06:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Spurred by the sell-off in the U.S. dollar on Tuesday morning, market participants are exiting long positions in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , nervous of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in the week to coincide with the expected Japanese government announcement on Thursday of plans to establish a "bridge bank" to take over the non-performing assets of ailing financial institutions. \par \par \uc2  With U.S. markets closed for a public holiday on Friday, some traders suspect that Asian central banks may take advantage of the reduced liquidity to attempt to strengthen the yen by selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people are trying to get out (of long dollar positions)," said the German bank trader. \par \par \uc2  The sell-off in the dollar against the yen has also strengthened other Asian-Pacific \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At 0345 GMT (11:45 p.m. Monday EDT) the Australian dollar is quoted at\par $0.6131, compared with $0.6090 around an hour and a half ago and $0.6072\par late the previous day in New York.\par    -By Tom Holland; 65 421 4800\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 30-06-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0408GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1074 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du6u05mki\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
