\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar slips on yen in Asia while dlr/mark steady. \b0\par\par\uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 5 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped around a yen in early Asian trading and extended the loss later, but volume was low in the absence of the Tokyo market and selling pressure was light, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen was coming down even in the face of a still firm mark/yen which was still well above the 74.50 yen level and expected to perk up to the 75.00 level soon. \par \par \uc2  "Mark/yen is still the best risk reward trade as far as we are concerned on a medium term basis," said Steve Brice, Regional Treasury Economist at Standard Chartered in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But he noted there was some concern about BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although probably not at these levels, and the very fact that a lot of investors are long mark/yen could mean any negative news from Germany or positive news from Japan could see it snap back. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/yen's slip certainly did not represent a huge shakeout and it bounced nicely at 132.60 and 132.50 would almost certainly hold if it tests the downside again, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there is a good chance that Europe will buy dollar/yen back up again from the sub-133 level. London traders return to their desks today after Monday's holiday. \par \par \uc2  There is good technical resistance at 133.50, where knock-out options exist, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The mark has shrugged aside the compromise deal on the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head and dollar/mark was holding steady around the New York close of 1.7790/95. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that technically, the mark rally has good reason to stall and it is approaching strong resistance levels. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar held steady after March retail sales data came in much in line with expectations at up 0.7 percent. \par \par \uc2  This followed a 1.0 percent drop in February and while analysts had seen this as an aberration there were some jitters after Monday's news of a record monthly trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said today's 0.7 percent rise together with elements of the trade data which showed some semblance of growth means the authorities are likely to hold hard on monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at US$0.6419/24 against US$0.6411/16 ahead of the data. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of Australia (RBA) meets today to discuss monetary policy but a Reuters poll showed that just 30 percent of economists surveyed expected an easing. \par \par \uc2  Governor Ian Macfarlane's semi-annual policy statement and testimony before a parliamentary committee is later this week. \par \par \uc2  Official Australian interest rates currently stand at 5.0 percent and were last cut by 0.5 point on July 30, 1997. \par \par \uc2  Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Desk +65-870-3840 \par \par \uc2  asia.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 @reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du5505zau\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
