\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  end mixed on domestic news. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Richard Hubbard \par \uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 1998\par 14:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 4 (Reuters) - Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  turned in mixed performances in subdued holiday-affected trade on Monday as the focus shifted to domestic developments around the region. \par \par \uc2  These included the aftershocks of the May Day riots in Korea, deepening concern over the future of Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor and news of another sharp rise in Indonesian inflation. \par \par \uc2  "Overall market players are sidelined just waiting to see what happens in Indonesia, " said Kenneth Cheng chief Asian analyst at MCM Asia Pacific. \par \par \uc2  Late in the day Indonesia reported a jump in its year-on-year inflation to 44.9 percent in April from 39.1 percent in March. \par \par \uc2  "It's in line with the trend. I think we're looking at hyperinflation numbers here," Cheng said. \par \par \uc2  But the Indonesian government earlier on Monday announced sharp hikes in fuel electricity and transport prices, which will feed into inflation in the coming months, and the army came out warning students about further anti-Suharto riots. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah has so far remained fairly stable as most market players are reluctant to get too involved. \par \par \uc2  In Thailand pressure was building on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor all day with rumours of his imminent resignation sweeping the markets. The news finally came near the close of trading when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, Chaiyawat Wibulswadi, announced he was going on May 5. \par \par \uc2  The baht had been slightly weaker all day in anticipation of the news. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won was an early target of sellers as concerns over labour instability continue to plague the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On Friday over 20,000 workers and students staged widespread rioting in the capital Seoul. \par \par \uc2  The riots led to condemnations by the government but also sparked a large sell off in Korean shares with the KOSPI index shedding some 3.6 percent on the day and putting further pressure on the won. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso bucked the trend to rise by over two percent on the day as foreign banks sold U.S. dollars for peso when it struck the 40 .39 level. \par \par \uc2  Some traders suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have had a hand in the action prompting firm denials from the Bank. \par \par \uc2  It said it had undertaken no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar strengthened slightly on reports the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  is continuing to look at the details of Singapore dollar internationalisation, one analyst said. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du54066iy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
