\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS-Regionals propped up but rises cappe. \b0\par\par\uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  0500 GMT - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stuck at early levels, see-sawing between strong yen and demand for cheap dollars from U.S. funds. \par \par \uc2  * The Indonesian rupiah firms slightly after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises its rupiah deposit rates by four percentage points. \par \par \uc2  * Dealers say the market failed to react to Bank Indonesia governor Sjahril Sabirin's overnight comment that he does not see another interest rate hike in the near term. \par \par \uc2  Sabirin told Reuters in an interview late on Thursday Bank Indonesia was following a dual policy to strengthen the rupiah - through interest rates and economic reform measures to boost confidence. \par \par \uc2  He also said fair value for the rupiah was at 5,000-6,000 to the dollar and he saw the Indonesian economy starting to rebound in the first quarter of next year. \par \par \uc2  * In Thailand, the Labour Ministry says the number of unemployed has jumped to 2.03 million, exceeding end-year projections, from 1.8 million at the end of 1997. \par \par \uc2  * The baht remains caught between offshore dollar sales and demand from importers and other companies. \par \par \uc2  * News of Bangkok Bank's new share issue at 93 baht per share is welcomed by the market, though most dealers say it has been largely priced in. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's top bank raised more than $1.0 billion on Thursday with the 440 million new share offering. \par \par \uc2  * The share issue is expected to generate offshore demand for the baht and dealers in Singapore say there is talk the Government of Singapore Investment Corp (GSIC) has been buying the baht in recent sessions. \par \par \uc2  A GSIC spokeswoman declines to comment on the rumours. \par \par \uc2  * Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says foreign reserves fell to US$83.615 billion at end March from $84.011 billion at end February. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say the fall could be due partly the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quiet defence of the Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  * The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also says broad M2 money supply grew 8.13 percent year-on-year in March after rising 8.51 percent in February. \par \par \uc2  * The Taiwan dollar remains above the T$33 level to the U.S. dollar on the yen's strength. \par \par \uc2  * Elsewhere, the Malaysian ringgit looks weak after failing to extend its gains much despite the stock market's rally on news of the merger between RHB Bank and Sime Bank. \par \par \uc2  * Interest is dampened by news Bank Negara Malaysia is to provide a 12-month guarantee against unforeseen liabilities of Sime Bank. \par \par \uc2  * "The inability of the ringgit to strengthen reflects investor concern over the Malaysian banking industry as a whole," said Banque Paribas strategist Thio Chin Loo. \par \par \uc2  "The lack of transparency over bank asset problems despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  assurances is unlikely to be improved in the near term and investors are expected to remain wary of further surprises around the corner," she said in a daily report. \par \par \uc2  * The ringgit is seen trading a 3.73-3.80 range to the dollar, with fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limiting its decline. \par \par \uc2  * The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar tracks a now familiar 1.58-1.60 range to the U.S. dollar, with activity largely driven by cross play against the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  * The Philippine peso remains weak after falling to 38.79 per dollar in early trade as dealers buy dollars to square their positions before leaving for a weekend \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  conference. \par \par \uc2  * The South Korean won gains from the yen's strength, but its rise is expected to be determined by the size of expected import deals. \par \par \uc2  * Finance Minister Lee Kyu-sung says South Korea urgently needs to lower interest rates no later than May. \par \par \uc2  Lee says in an advance copy of a speech that high interest rates threaten to push many economically viable companies into bankruptcy, which would lead to rising unemployment. \par \par \uc2  But he implies that any move to lower interest rates would only be with the blessing of the IMF. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4o06wex\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
