\par\par \b \uc2  SE Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  -2: Enthusiasm Wanes At Anwar's Speech \b0\par\par\uc2 268 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par 09:36\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Following Monday's rally, which saw the ringgit break up\par through a key resistance level at MYR3.60 to the U.S. dollar\par in anticipation of market-friendly reforms in Malaysia,\par traders and investors reined in their enthusiasm.\par    "The market is very cautious ahead of Anwar's speech, no\par one has any intention of pushing the ringgit higher for the\par time being," said a regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader at a French\par bank in Singapore, adding that the Malaysian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was\par seeing good selling interest at levels around 3.55 ringgit\par to the U.S. dollar.\par    Anwar's revised forecast for Malaysian economic growth\par in 1998 proved in line with market expectations at 2% to 3%,\par compared with the government's previous target of 4% to 5%.\par No increase in the foreign ownership limits on Malaysian\par banks was announced, however, to the disappointment of some\par market players.\par    At 0820 GMT (3:20 a.m. EST) the U.S. dollar was quoted\par at MYR3.6200, up from MYR3.5550 late in Asian trading on\par Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The Singapore dollar was also sold off ahead of Anwar's\par parliamentary speech.\par    A combination of U.S. dollar purchases from local\par corporations and market fears of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the island republic's de\par facto \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , led market participants to cut back\par their long Singapore dollar positions said traders.\par    The U.S. dollar was quoted at 0820 GMT at S$1.6075, up\par from S$1.5995 late on Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires  03-24-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0336EST\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du3o00osc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
